title: Reloading the configuration
tags: [[Customising the GUI]] Configuration
kind: reference
aliases: reload config-reload hot-reload restart
summary: config:reload re-reads the keybindings, the style sheet, the query grammar and commands.js while the GUI runs; the rest of the configuration is read at startup, and why.

`config:reload <what>` reads a part of the configuration again, without a restart.

|!`what` |!Reads |!Notes |
|`keybindings` |`gui/keybindings.toml` |for a file edited outside the GUI: the settings view applies its own changes. Keys suggested by a panel or by `commands.js`, and the temporary keys of a running script, are kept ([[Changing keybindings]]) |
|`style` |`gui/style.css` |also done automatically when the file is saved ([[Style and theming]]) |
|`grammar` |`core/query-grammar` |the simplified query language ([[Editing the grammar]]) |
|`commands` |`gui/commands.js` |the commands of the previous version are forgotten first ([[User commands]]) |
|`all` |the four, in this order | |

The status bar reports what was reloaded. A file that cannot be read or is malformed
is refused with the reason, and what was in effect stays. With `all`, the parts
before the failing one are reloaded and the ones after it are not.

Another name is refused with the list of the accepted ones.

!! What is not reloadable

Three parts of the configuration are left out on purpose.

* ''`core/ignore-presets.toml`'' needs no reload: it is read each time an `ignore:*` command runs.
* ''`gui/config.toml`'' is read at startup, and much of it is used up then: `gui-port` is the port the GUI's server is listening on, the timings of `[settings]` were given to background tasks when they started, and the page sizes, panel timings and `[panel-defaults]` are handed to each panel when it is created. Reloading only what could be would leave the GUI in a state that no configuration file describes; a restart is asked for instead ([[GUI configuration]]).
* ''`gui/panel-types/`'' — the files are read from disk each time they are asked for, but the web view keeps a JavaScript module once it is loaded, and a panel, once created, lives until the GUI exits. An edited panel is picked up at the next start ([[Panel types]]).
